{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.domain`\n\nThe {term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain` implements\n`trust_but_verify::domain::Domain` to define its node-identity type. Its\nerror type is introduced in {cite}`requirements.sdk.errors`:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    /// The *identifier* identifies a particular node in the graph.\n    type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.domain_types` has one obligation. An application domain implements `Domain` and defines `type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. In practice this means two things: the associated type must carry exactly those bounds, and an application can supply its own node-identity type.\n\n- **Exact bounds.** The test `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]` covers this.\n  - It implements `Domain` for a fixture whose `NodeId` is `Identity`. `Identity` derives only `Clone`, `PartialEq` and `Eq`, so it has no `Debug`, `Hash` or `Ord`. If the trait added any further bound, the fixture would fail to compile.\n  - Its generic function `check_bounds<G: Domain>` calls `identity::<G::NodeId>()`, which requires `Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. If the trait dropped any of those bounds, this would also fail to compile.\n  - Together these catch both loosening and tightening of the bound.\n- **Node identity in use.** The same test calls `node_path`, `outgoing_edges`, `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` with the opaque identity.\n  - It asserts that `node_path(&Identity(1))` differs from `node_path(&Identity(2))`.\n  - It checks that edges return the expected `Identity` values, including duplicates.\n  - It checks that storage futures stay `Send`.\n- **Alternative `NodeId` shapes.** Other tests show that different `NodeId` types work.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[enum_node_ids_distinguish_requirements_and_source_items_in_one_domain]` uses an enum `NodeId` with variants of different shape. It asserts that the variants are unequal, that their paths differ, that dependencies record the ids, and that evidence is stored and loaded per id.\n  - `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[habeas_paths_preserve_opaque_segments_and_separate_all_node_kinds]` and `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[review_graph_delegates_unprepared_sources_and_paths_to_the_domain]` exercise the real `HabeasNode` identity type.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs` and `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` use `NodeId` through the full validation flow.\n\nI found no obligation or boundary that the supplied tests leave unexercised.",
  "references": "8f371c4ec7f0c0885da5414a2e1353bd9a2d1a595b7e6b3d293f799812f87832",
